$DIR/main.p:5:22: main.(*O).Drop keeps o past the call, but the owner it points to is destroyed when Drop returns
    A Drop method's receiver points to the owner being dropped: its fields are
    dropped and its memory released when Drop returns (language spec section
    18.6, What dropping does). Keeping o, or a part of the owner, in package
    memory, the heap, a channel or a task leaves a view of memory that is gone.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Copy the values Drop needs to keep out of the owner, rather than keeping a
    pointer into it
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3002" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:9:1: cannot pass &o to (*O).Drop (called when o is dropped), which keeps it after returning
    &o is a pointer to o. o is a local variable of f (declared at main.p:7).
    (*O).Drop (called when o is dropped) stores o in package variable keep
    (main.p:5), so &o would outlive o.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    If (*O).Drop (called when o is dropped) should keep its own copy of o,
    change it to take the value, not a pointer, or an owner it can keep: an
    owned[O] from new, a shared[O], or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)
